Observed grand total: 35. ✓

ADR 0009's four canonical-form groups land as four named patterns —
p_0 (3-cycle), p_1 (single edge), p_2 (3-spoke out-star), p_3 (2-edge
forward chain). Every prediction matches the oracle.

====================================================================
OBSERVATIONS
====================================================================

1. The three-shape encoding holds tight.
   Every piece of information ADR 0010 commits to storing — pattern
   existence, instance ownership, participant membership — lands as
   a plain R instance. No new relation kinds, no position markers,
   no synthetic edge identifiers. Commitments 1 (only R) and 3
   (types as meta-R) are both honored without accommodation.

2. p_1 dominates the registry at six instances.
   The known "single edge" pattern class from ADR 0009 becomes one
   named pattern with six instances. This is empirically the
   strongest argument for leaving the relevance filter to γ: naming
   every canonical class will frequently surface trivial patterns
   that add little explanatory value. "Which patterns deserve
   naming" needs a policy, and the γ layer is where that policy
   lives.

3. RSet size grew from 14 to 49 edges after naming 9 instances.
   2.5× growth on a trivial graph. At scale, the growth factor
   depends on participant count per instance; stars grow the
   ownership tree quickly, while short chains grow it slowly. The
   growth itself is intentional — it is commitment 3 made visible.
   γ will have to decide whether periodic rebuilds, cleanup, or
   generational stores are needed for longer-running experiments.

4. Reverse-derivability held.
   `find_pattern_matching` relies on reconstructing each instance's
   canonical form from participants plus RSet edges. Inside
   `name_pattern_instances`, this derivation is queried twice: once
   to dedupe the first instance against existing patterns (for the
   chain canonical `[(1, 2), (2, 0)]`, no prior match, mint fresh),
   and repeatedly during the example's four naming calls. Every
   lookup succeeded — the invariant "instance participants do not
   acquire new edges among themselves" holds trivially on this
   single-pass run.

5. Instance participant sets are symmetric.
   p_0_i_0 lists `{k1, k2, k3}` without direction or position
   information; the edges recoverable from those three identifiers
   encode the rotational cycle. Likewise p_3_i_0's `{c2, c3, c4}`
   decodes to a 2-edge chain. The participants-only encoding is
   structurally sufficient because canonicalization re-derives
   direction from the underlying edges.

====================================================================
WHAT THIS ENABLES / WHAT IT DOES NOT
====================================================================

Enables:
  - A durable record of discovered patterns, stored in the same R
    primitive as the data they describe (commitment 3).
  - O(1) membership queries: `instances_of(pattern)`,
    `participants_of(instance)`, `patterns()`.
  - Rebuild of a pattern's canonical form on demand; no canonical
    form stored means no drift between encoding and code.
  - A foundation for γ to decide which patterns to actually name.

Does NOT enable (explicitly out of scope for ADR 0010):
  - Automatic discovery. A caller still has to invoke
    `name_pattern_instances`. ADR 0011 (γ) will walk the pipeline
    and pick candidates.
  - Relevance or triviality filtering. p_1's "every single edge"
    pattern is useless but gets named by this ADR as-is.
  - Pattern retirement / cleanup. Once named, patterns persist.
  - Recursive discovery. The feedback loop is noted but not acted
    on — the pipeline is not re-run against the enlarged RSet.
    Re-running would find new compound classes involving
    `__pattern__`, `p_N`, `p_N_i_M` nodes, potentially naming
    patterns-of-patterns. γ decides.

====================================================================
DECISION / NEXT
====================================================================

ADR 0010 is validated. The β layer now has:
  - extraction (0008)
  - canonicalization (0009)
  - naming (0010, this ADR)

Only γ (ADR 0011) remains of the β plan. Its scope:
  - Policy for which candidate patterns to name (size ≥ k? edges
    ≥ m? MDL? non-triviality filter?).
  - Iteration: does the system re-run the pipeline against the
    enlarged RSet after naming? If so, under what stopping
    condition?
  - Integrity: does γ defend the "participants don't gain new
    edges among themselves" invariant, or relax it with a
    stored-canonical fallback?
  - Trigger: when does γ run at all? On every new R instance?
    Batched? On demand?

Worth running one more probe experiment before ADR 0011 is
scoped: what does the enlarged RSet (post-naming) look like to
ADR 0007's compound_class_subgraphs? Does it surface the
`__pattern__`/`p_N`/`p_N_i_M` tokens as new structural neighbors
in ways that suggest natural patterns-of-patterns? If yes, γ's
iteration policy has a concrete target.
